was a mastermind of fear and brutality  he helped osama bin laden turn al-qaeda  into a global network of terror from the  safe haven of afghanistan when it was  first under taliban control  he's really been at the helm of jihad  for  at least 40 years if not 50 years  so  he's he's got that whole cocktail of  respectable qualities what he really  lacks  is the kind of charisma that  osama bin laden had i think he lacks an  ability to  attract a new young generation  working together the pair unleashed  carnage on the u.s embassies in tanzania  and kenya  horror on the uss coal off the coast of  yemen  and then the bloodiest attack of them  all against new york and washington on  the 11th of september 2001.  the atrocities made this egyptian doctor  one of the most wanted terrorists on the  planet along with the then al-qaeda  chief bin laden  both men had 25 million dollar bounties  on their heads  when bin laden was finally found and  killed in 2011 zawahiri was his obvious  successor  but he lacked the same visionary appeal  and al-qaeda's ability to control the  actions of an array of terror franchises  faulted  the rise of rival group islamic state  also posed a challenge  that said last year's u.s withdrawal  from afghanistan did throw al qaeda a  lifeline  a resurgent taliban appears willing to  offer the group a haven in defiance of  u.s demands  yet it's hard to imagine whoever takes  over from zawahiri will achieve the same  level of notoriety as he did with bin  laden those in the running include  egyptian militant safe al-adha abdul  rathman al-mughrabi from morocco or one  of the leaders of two al-qaeda  affiliates yazid mubarak or ahmed durie  it does close the chapter on this  particular expression of the kind of  terrorist threat but you know terrorism  unfortunately is a concept and as a  practice has been around for a very long  time what we've seen is that it tends to  morph into new directions and  unfortunately that's probably what we're  going to see now  taking out the leader of a terrorist  group is clearly a key moment  but it's no long-term fix  debra haynes sky news
 

